<h1>SPECTRA — Project Architecture & Module 4 Deep-Dive</h1>
<div class="header-meta">
  <strong>Project:</strong> SPECTRA (AI-Driven PQC Reverse Proxy for Zero-Trust) &nbsp;|&nbsp;
  <strong>Lead Module:</strong> M4 — AI Risk Engine &nbsp;|&nbsp;
  <strong>Dataset:</strong> UNSW-NB15 Benchmark &nbsp;|&nbsp;
  <strong>Status:</strong> Phase 1 Final Defense Ready
</div>

<div class="card card-success">
  <strong>Document Purpose:</strong> Comprehensive technical guide and viva defense reference for SPECTRA and Module 4 (AI Risk Engine). Every metric, threshold, architectural boundary, and code implementation detail is verified against the finalized codebase.
</div>

<h2>1. System Abstract & The Big Picture</h2>

<h3>The Problem in Traditional Networks</h3>
<p>Modern enterprise networks face two distinct existential security threats:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The Quantum Threat ("Harvest Now, Decrypt Later")</strong>: Traditional TLS relies on RSA and ECC. Adversaries are actively capturing and archiving encrypted enterprise network traffic today. Once a Cryptographically Relevant Quantum Computer (CRQC) emerges, Shor's algorithm will crack RSA and ECC retrospectively, decrypting years of historical corporate secrets.</li>
  <li><strong>Broken Perimeters & Lateral Movement</strong>: Traditional networks authenticate users once at the perimeter (VPN). Once inside, the network implicitly trusts the session. If an attacker steals a token or compromises an endpoint, they can move laterally across internal databases undetected.</li>
</ol>

<h3>What SPECTRA Does</h3>
<p><strong>SPECTRA</strong> (<strong>S</strong>ecure <strong>P</strong>ost-quantum <strong>E</strong>nforcement &amp; <strong>C</strong>ontinuous <strong>T</strong>elemetry <strong>R</strong>isk <strong>A</strong>nalysis) is a Zero-Trust Reverse Proxy that terminates client connections at the network edge in front of protected backend microservices.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Transport Security (M1)</strong>: Enforces post-quantum lattice cryptography (NIST ML-KEM and ML-DSA) with hardware-aware dynamic parameter scaling.</li>
  <li><strong>Edge Routing &amp; Enforcement (M2)</strong>: Intercepts every individual request on port <code>8443</code>, routing verified requests to backend port <code>9000</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Telemetry Extraction (M3)</strong>: Leverages Linux in-kernel <strong>eBPF probes</strong> to extract non-intrusive flow statistics without user-space buffer copies.</li>
  <li><strong>Intelligence Plane (M4)</strong>: Evaluates flow telemetry using an unsupervised <strong>Isolation Forest</strong> with <strong>empirical normal percentile calibration</strong>, outputting continuous risk scores in <code>[0.0, 1.0]</code> within 5.7 milliseconds.</li>
</ul>

<pre><code>[Client: Web / Mobile / IoT]
           │
           ▼  (1) Quantum-Safe Handshake: ML-KEM / ML-DSA Negotiation
[M1: Adaptive PQC Transport Layer]
           │
           ▼  (2) Inbound Request Terminated at Edge Proxy (Port 8443)
[M2: Zero-Trust Reverse Proxy Engine]
           │
           ├──► (3) Non-intrusive socket & packet monitoring (eBPF)
           │    [M3: IDS & Telemetry Extraction Probe]
           │             │
           │             ▼ (4) 4-tuple vector: [packet_size, rate, conns, sinpkt]
           │    [M4: AI Risk Engine]
           │             │
           │             ▼ (5) Isolation Forest decision_function()
           │             ▼ (6) Empirical Percentile Calibration Lookup
           │             │
           ◄─────────────┘ (7) Returns Risk Score ∈ [0.0, 1.0]
[M2: Policy Enforcement Gate]
           │
           ├─► Risk &lt; 0.970       ──► ALLOW: Injects X-Spectra-Verified ──► [Internal Microservice:9000]
           ├─► 0.970 ≤ Risk &lt; 0.995 ──► CHALLENGE: Triggers Step-Up MFA  ──► [Client Auth Challenge]
           └─► Risk ≥ 0.995       ──► DROP: Severs Socket (HTTP 403)     ──► [Connection Dropped]</code></pre>

