<h2>3. Deep-Dive: Module 4 (AI Risk Engine)</h2>

<h3>Why Unsupervised Anomaly Detection?</h3>
<p>Traditional signature systems fail against zero-day exploits. Supervised machine learning models fail in network security due to the <strong>labeling bottleneck</strong> (zero-day attacks cannot be labeled in advance). M4 is strictly <strong>unsupervised</strong>: it is trained <strong>exclusively on normal baseline traffic (<code>label == 0</code>)</strong>. It learns the mathematical density of benign traffic so that any abnormal outlier is immediately flagged.</p>

<h3>The 4 Network-Flow Features</h3>
<p>M4 represents each network observation as a 4-dimensional continuous vector:</p>
<pre><code>x = [packet_size, request_rate, connection_count, inter_arrival_time]</code></pre>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature Name</th>
      <th>UNSW-NB15 Column</th>
      <th>Physical Meaning</th>
      <th>Normal Example</th>
      <th>Anomalous Example</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>packet_size</code></td>
      <td><code>smean</code></td>
      <td>Mean byte size per transmitted packet.</td>
      <td><code>520 B</code> (Standard HTTP GET)</td>
      <td><code>9,500 B</code> (Buffer overflow / data exfil)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>request_rate</code></td>
      <td><code>rate</code></td>
      <td>Transmitted requests / packets per second.</td>
      <td><code>6.0 req/s</code> (Human browsing)</td>
      <td><code>300.0 req/s</code> (Automated flood / fuzzer)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>connection_count</code></td>
      <td><code>ct_srv_src</code></td>
      <td>Concurrent active sockets to same service.</td>
      <td><code>3.0</code> (Normal browser sockets)</td>
      <td><code>80.0</code> (Reconnaissance / DoS probe)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>inter_arrival_time</code></td>
      <td><code>sinpkt / 1000.0</code></td>
      <td>Time interval between consecutive packets (sec).</td>
      <td><code>0.180 s</code> (Human pacing)</td>
      <td><code>0.002 s</code> (Automated script saturation)</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Isolation Forest Mechanics</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Core Principle</strong>: "Anomalies are few and different." Outliers in high-dimensional space require far fewer random splits to isolate than normal points packed in dense clusters.</li>
  <li><strong>Tree Construction</strong>: Builds an ensemble of 100 binary trees (<code>n_estimators = 100</code>). Each tree recursively partitions feature space by randomly choosing a feature and split value.</li>
  <li><strong>Path Length to Score</strong>:
    <ul>
      <li>Dense normal points $\rightarrow$ Deep path length (Depth 10–14) $\rightarrow$ <code>decision_function &gt; 0</code>.</li>
      <li>Sparse anomalies $\rightarrow$ Shallow path length (Depth 1–4) $\rightarrow$ <code>decision_function &lt; 0</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Efficiency</strong>: Linear time complexity $O(n)$ with inference execution in <strong>~5.7 ms</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Empirical Percentile Calibration</h3>
<p>Rather than using arbitrary sigmoid normalization ($1 / (1 + e^{-k \cdot x})$), M4 calibrates raw scores against <strong>11,200 held-out normal samples</strong> that the model never saw during tree fitting.</p>
<ol>
  <li>In training (<code>src/train_model.py</code>), anomaly scores of held-out normal traffic are calculated and sorted:
    <code>anomaly_scores = -model.decision_function(X_calibration)</code></li>
  <li>In inference (<code>src/m4_interface.py</code>), the incoming score is ranked via binary search:
    <code>position = np.searchsorted(REFERENCE_ANOMALY_SCORES, anomaly_score, side="right")</code></li>
  <li>The percentile risk is:
    <code>Risk = position / 11200 ∈ [0.0, 1.0]</code></li>
</ol>

<div class="card card-info">
  <strong>Precise Meaning of Risk = 0.970:</strong><br>
  A risk score of 0.970 means: <em>"This telemetry vector is more anomalous than approximately 97.0% of verified normal baseline traffic."</em> It is an empirical percentile rank, not an attack probability.
</div>

<h3>Calibrated Zero-Trust Policy Thresholds</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>ALLOW (Risk &lt; 0.970)</strong>: Normal transaction. Injects <code>X-Spectra-Verified</code> header and forwards to backend.</li>
  <li><strong>MFA / MONITOR (0.970 ≤ Risk &lt; 0.995)</strong>: Elevated anomaly. Triggers step-up authentication. Setting this at 0.970 mathematically guarantees that at most ~3% of legitimate normal traffic will ever be challenged.</li>
  <li><strong>DROP (Risk ≥ 0.995)</strong>: Extreme anomaly. Corresponds to the upper 0.5% of the calibrated normal reference distribution. Severs TCP connection immediately with HTTP 403.</li>
</ul>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Risk Score</th>
      <th>Raw Decision Score</th>
      <th>Empirical Distribution Position</th>
      <th>Enforced Policy Action</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>0.070</strong></td>
      <td><code>+0.1546</code></td>
      <td>Deep in normal cluster (7th percentile).</td>
      <td><strong>ALLOW</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>0.850</strong></td>
      <td><code>+0.0410</code></td>
      <td>Normal operational variance (85th percentile).</td>
      <td><strong>ALLOW</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>0.980</strong></td>
      <td><code>-0.0150</code></td>
      <td>Elevated deviation; exceeds 98% of normal baseline.</td>
      <td><strong>MFA / MONITOR</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>1.000</strong></td>
      <td><code>-0.2144</code></td>
      <td>Exceeds 100% of normal baseline calibration samples.</td>
      <td><strong>DROP</strong></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

