<h2>7. Master Viva Defense Preparation</h2>

<h3>The Elevator Pitches</h3>

<div class="card">
  <h4>30-Second Elevator Pitch (The Entire Project)</h4>
  <p><em>"SPECTRA is an AI-driven, Post-Quantum Zero-Trust Reverse Proxy designed to secure internal microservices against both future quantum decryption and current lateral network movement. At the transport layer, it implements hardware-adaptive NIST PQC primitives (ML-KEM and ML-DSA). At the policy layer, it continuously scores network-flow telemetry using an unsupervised Isolation Forest calibrated with empirical percentiles, dynamically enforcing step-up MFA or connection dropping with sub-10ms inference latency."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h4>1-Minute Pitch (The Entire Project)</h4>
  <p><em>"Traditional perimeter security suffers from two critical flaws: legacy public-key encryption like RSA and ECC is vulnerable to 'Harvest Now, Decrypt Later' quantum attacks, and static single-login perimeters fail once an adversary bypasses the boundary. SPECTRA addresses both. Incoming connections negotiate NIST-standardized quantum-safe algorithms—ML-KEM for key encapsulation and ML-DSA for digital signatures—adapting dynamically to client compute capacity. Behind this transport layer, an edge reverse proxy coordinates with eBPF kernel probes to extract flow telemetry. This telemetry is evaluated by an unsupervised Isolation Forest that maps flow vectors against empirical normal percentiles. Sessions with normal risk are permitted, moderate deviations trigger MFA challenges, and extreme anomalies are severed at the edge—delivering continuous verification with minimal latency."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="card card-info">
  <h4>2-Minute Pitch (Module 4 Deep-Dive — Your Module)</h4>
  <p><em>"I am responsible for Module 4: the AI Risk Engine. Traditional intrusion detection relies on static signatures or supervised models, which fail against novel zero-day attacks and suffer from severe labeling bottlenecks. In M4, I implemented an unsupervised anomaly detection engine powered by an Isolation Forest, trained exclusively on normal network traffic across four core flow features: packet size, request rate, connection count, and inter-arrival time. Rather than applying arbitrary sigmoid transformations, I engineered an empirical percentile calibration mechanism. We train the model on 80% of normal traffic and calibrate it against a held-out 20% normal baseline. When a live telemetry vector is evaluated, M4 calculates its exact percentile rank against this held-out distribution. This enables mathematically grounded zero-trust policy thresholds: traffic below the 97th percentile is allowed; traffic between the 97th and 99.5th percentiles triggers step-up MFA; and extreme outliers in the top 0.5% are dropped. I benchmarked M4 against the official 82,332-sample UNSW-NB15 dataset. The engine achieved 83.12% precision and a 4.42% false-positive rate, with a median inference latency of 5.72 ms. While volumetric attacks were detected effectively, stealthy payload exploits confirmed that pure flow features alone cannot catch single-packet attacks—providing empirical justification for our Phase 2 multi-modal telemetry fusion."</em></p>
</div>

<h3>Top 10 High-Yield Viva Questions &amp; Answers</h3>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q1: What does "unsupervised" mean in M4, and how is it trained?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer:</strong> The model is trained <strong>strictly on normal baseline traffic (<code>label == 0</code>)</strong>. It is never fed attack signatures or labels during training. It learns what normal traffic looks like so that any statistical outlier is flagged as an anomaly.</div>
  <div class="qa-followup"><strong>Examiner follow-up:</strong> <em>"What is the contamination parameter for?"</em> $\rightarrow$ <em>"In scikit-learn, <code>contamination=0.05</code> sets the offset for the raw decision boundary, assuming ~5% of baseline network records contain mild noise."</em></div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q2: How do you convert raw Isolation Forest scores into a 0 to 1 risk score?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer:</strong> We negate <code>model.decision_function(X)</code> so higher values represent greater anomalies, then execute <code>np.searchsorted()</code> against our sorted array of 11,200 held-out normal anomaly scores. The index divided by 11,200 gives the empirical percentile risk score in $[0.0, 1.0]$.</div>
  <div class="qa-followup"><strong>Examiner follow-up:</strong> <em>"What is the physical meaning of a 0.970 score?"</em> $\rightarrow$ <em>"It means the observation is more anomalous than 97.0% of verified normal traffic."</em></div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q3: Why are your thresholds set to 0.970 and 0.995 instead of 0.50 or 0.70?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer:</strong> Because in an empirical percentile model, normal traffic is uniformly distributed across $[0.0, 1.0]$. A 0.50 threshold would challenge 50% of legitimate users. Setting MFA at 0.970 mathematically bounds false positive challenges on normal traffic to ~3%. Setting DROP at 0.995 reserves termination strictly for the upper 0.5% extreme anomalies.</div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q4: Why is your recall only 17.78% on UNSW-NB15? Is that acceptable?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer (CRITICAL HONEST ANSWER):</strong> Recall is 17.78% because the prototype analyzes <strong>only 4 network-flow features</strong>. Single-packet exploits (SQLi, buffer overflows) travel in standard-sized packets at normal speed, mimicking normal flow headers. While low, this prototype successfully catches volumetric and flood attacks (Generic at 37.8%) with zero signatures, and empirically proves why Phase 2's cross-layer fusion with Suricata IDS signatures is required.</div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q5: What does an ROC-AUC of 0.7570 indicate?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer:</strong> It proves solid continuous discriminative power. If you randomly select one attack record and one normal record from the 82,332 test samples, the model will correctly rank the attack record as more anomalous 75.7% of the time.</div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q6: Why did you split normal data into training and calibration sets?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer:</strong> To avoid overfitting. Calibrating percentiles on the same data used to construct the isolation trees would yield overconfident percentiles. Using a held-out 20% normal split guarantees unbiased calibration.</div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q7: Why is M4 inference latency (~5.7 ms) so critical?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer:</strong> M4 sits in the inline evaluation path of a reverse proxy. If inference took 150 ms, every page asset would suffer extreme delays. A 5.7 ms median latency fits comfortably within modern zero-trust SLA budgets.</div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q8: Is M4 currently integrated with M2 in live real-time?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer (TRAP QUESTION):</strong> In the current Phase 1 codebase, M2 and M4 are validated as <strong>standalone modules</strong>. M2 currently uses a static risk score stub of <code>0.12</code> during proxy execution. Live dynamic HTTP/gRPC querying between M2 and M4 is designed and planned for Phase 2.</div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q9: Why use an Isolation Forest instead of One-Class SVM or Autoencoders?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer:</strong> Isolation Forest exhibits linear time complexity $O(n)$, low memory requirements, and sub-10ms inference. One-Class SVM scales quadratically ($O(n^2)$) and Autoencoders require GPU hardware and intensive tuning that introduce latency.</div>
</div>

<div class="qa-block">
  <div class="qa-q">Q10: How does SPECTRA mitigate the "Harvest Now, Decrypt Later" threat?</div>
  <div class="qa-a"><strong>Answer:</strong> By using NIST-standardized Post-Quantum Cryptography (ML-KEM and ML-DSA) at the transport layer, ensuring that intercepted ciphertext cannot be decrypted by Shor's algorithm on a quantum computer.</div>
</div>

